.TH PULL 1
.SH NAME
pull, replica/httpfs \- update the system from its update server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pull
[
.B -nSv
]
[
.B -c
.I path
]...
[
.B -s
.I path
]...
[
.I replica
]
.br
.B replica/httpfs
[
.B -D
]
[
.B -m
.I mtpt
]
[
.B -s
.I srvname
]
[
.B -w
.I webmtpt
]
.B -k
.I pubkey
.I url
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Pull
brings the installed system up to date from nfront's update server:
it runs
.I replica/pull
(see
.IR replica (1))
with the replica the install CD put in
.BR /dist/replica/nfront ,
or with
.IR replica .
The CD's replica names the server's URL, its public key
.RB ( /dist/replica/nfront.pub )
and the client's db and log
.RB ( /dist/replica/client ),
as of the CD: the installed system starts up to date.
Files the system configures
.RB ( /adm ,
.BR /cfg ,
.BR /cron ,
.BR /lib/ndb ,
.BR /mail ,
.BR /sys/log ,
.BR /usr )
are not replicated.
A file changed on the installed system is not overwritten:
.I replica/pull
reports a conflict and leaves it;
.B -c
.I path
keeps the client's version,
.B -s
.I path
takes the server's.
.B -n
shows what would change,
.B -v
says what changes.
.PP
On a
.IR gefs (4)
root file system,
.I pull
first takes a snapshot of
.BR main ,
named
.BI pull. YYYYMMDD.hhmmss\fR,
the way back: the files as they were are in
.BI /n/dump/pull. YYYYMMDD.hhmmss
after
.B 9fs
.BR dump .
Taking it needs a member of the
.B adm
group; without it
.I pull
stops, and
.B -S
pulls without a snapshot.
Other file systems have no snapshot: what
.I pull
replaces is gone.
.PP
.I Replica/httpfs
serves a replica's server tree published over HTTP, read-only, at
.I mtpt
(default
.BR /n/replica ),
for a replica's
.B servermount
function.
At
.I url
it reads
.BR index ,
the tree's files with their mode, owner, group, time, length and SHA-256,
and
.BR index.sig ,
the index's RSA signature (PKCS #1 v1.5, SHA-256),
and checks the signature with the PEM
.B RSA PUBLIC KEY
in
.IR pubkey ;
it does not start when it does not verify.
A file's contents come from
.IB url /obj/ xx / sha256
as it is read, through
.IR webfs (4)
at
.I webmtpt
(default
.BR /mnt/web );
the read that would end a file checks the SHA-256 of all of it and fails
when it differs from the index, so
.I replica/applylog
(which spools a file before it installs it) installs only files the
signed index names.
.I Webfs
does not check TLS certificates: the signature is what makes the server
trustworthy, over HTTP as over HTTPS.
.B -s
posts the server as
.BI /srv/ srvname\fR;
.B -D
traces 9P.
.SH FILES
.TF /dist/replica/client/nfront.db
.TP
.B /dist/replica/nfront
the replica: URL, key, client db and log
.TP
.B /dist/replica/nfront.pub
the update server's public key
.TP
.B /dist/replica/client/nfront.db
what the system has of the replica
.TP
.B /dist/replica/client/nfront.log
the server's log as far as the system has it
.SH SOURCE
.B /rc/bin/pull
.br
.B /sys/src/cmd/replica/httpfs.c
.br
the server side, on the build host:
.B scripts/replica/publish.py
of nfront's tree
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR replica (1),
.IR replica (8),
.IR gefs (4),
.IR gefs (8),
.IR webfs (4)
.SH BUGS
The index carries no expiry: a server, or one between, can keep handing
out an older signed index, which holds a system back but cannot give it
files the publisher did not sign.
